CLR (pinned commit 2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350, therock-7.14
/ ROCm 7.14.0): remove a hardcoded rejection of every GFX8 device from
the OpenCL (non-HIP) path, and stop letting an image-extension query
failure abort the entire device's initialization.

WHY
---
Independent of the ROCR-Runtime doorbell fix (see
hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch, needed first for the agent to
be created at all), CLR's own device layer has a second, unconditional
block specific to this repo's target:

    bool runtimeRocSupported() const {
      if (!IS_HIP && (versionMajor_ == 8)) {
        return false;
      }
      return runtimeRocSupported_;
    }

`device.hpp`'s `Isa::runtimeRocSupported()` rejects every ISA with
versionMajor_ == 8 outright for the OpenCL path (IS_HIP is false there),
regardless of what the per-ISA `runtimeRocSupported_` table already says
-- gfx803's own table entry already carries `runtimeRocSupported_=true`;
this extra `versionMajor_==8` gate throws that away specifically for
OpenCL. HIP is unaffected (IS_HIP short-circuits the check), which is
why this repo's existing gfx900+/gfx803 HIP-only builds never had to
touch this -- it only matters if OpenCL (`onnxruntime`'s MIGraphX/rocBLAS
path doesn't use it, but `clinfo`/general OpenCL consumers do, and it's
part of validating "does the runtime actually work" beyond just HIP) is
in scope.

Separately, `rocdevice.cpp`'s `populateOCLDeviceConstants()` queries a
run of HSA image-extension attributes (`HSA_EXT_AGENT_INFO_MAX_SAMPLER_
HANDLERS`, `..._MAX_IMAGE_RD_HANDLES`, `..._IMAGE_2D_MAX_ELEMENTS`, etc.)
and returns `false` -- aborting device initialization entirely -- the
moment any one of them fails. On gfx803 these HSA image-extension
queries fail (the extension either isn't populated for this legacy ISA
or the underlying data isn't there), which without this patch takes down
the whole OpenCL device, not just image support specifically.

Both confirmed via lucbruni-amd's (AMD engineer) from-source TheRock
build verified on real Polaris hardware (RX 550): "rocminfo and clinfo
both detect gfx803 (RX 550) successfully" -- see
https://github.com/lucbruni-amd/TheRock/commit/3d4ad6093a0069876fbfff590bb56ddb340ae9c5
(branch lb/gfx803-polaris-support),
patches/gfx803/rocm-systems/0002-clr-Enable-OpenCL-support-for-gfx8-Polaris-GPUs.patch
within that commit. Reproduced here, re-diffed clean against this repo's
own pinned rocm-systems commit (2b22ab0195, therock-7.14) --
`git apply --check` passes with no fuzz.

WHAT
----
- device.hpp: delete the `!IS_HIP && versionMajor_ == 8` early-return,
  so OpenCL falls through to the same `runtimeRocSupported_` table value
  HIP already uses.
- rocdevice.cpp: track image-query success in a local `image_queries_ok`
  flag instead of returning `false` from the function on the first
  failure; only the fields whose own query failed go unpopulated, image
  support is disabled gracefully (`info_.imageSupport_ = false` with a
  `LogWarning`) if any query failed, and every other OpenCL device
  capability (compute, buffers, atomics, etc.) still initializes
  normally.
Applies to: CLR @ rocm-systems release/therock-10.0 (commit 6b0e43f341,
2026-08-29). Re-diffed from the 7.14-era version (which pinned
2b22ab0195cc1461cd9abf3b969e9dd7c10af350): device.hpp moved up a directory
(device/rocm/device.hpp -> device/device.hpp, now the shared CLR device
header) and populateOCLDeviceConstants()'s image-query block shifted to
~1463; both target hunks confirmed present at 10.0 and the intent is
unchanged. One upstream change is preserved rather than reverted: the
imagePitchAlignment_/imageBaseAddressAlignment_/bufferFromImageSupport_
assignments moved OUT of the image_is_supported block upstream (set
unconditionally, per the PAL-backend comment) -- this patch keeps them
where 10.0 has them and only removes the return-false-on-image-query-failure
behavior. Needs hsa-agent-rejects-legacy-doorbell.patch applied first for
the HSA agent underneath to exist at all -- this patch only affects what
CLR does once that agent is already valid. See
opencl-gfx8-hardcoded-rejection.sh for how this is applied and verified at
build time.

